  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>The 48 hours from 2 to 4 June were defined by two simultaneous arcs running in opposite directions: a Lebanon de-escalation track and a broader Iran–US–regional military escalation track, with the Hormuz situation unchanged in its de facto closure. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On the escalation arc, the overnight of 2–3 June produced the heaviest exchange of strikes since the April ceasefire framework. Iranian drones and ballistic missiles hit Kuwait International Airport (one person killed, terminal damaged, flights suspended) and targeted US 5th Fleet facilities in Bahrain; the US military struck an Iranian ground-control station at Bandar Abbas and the IRGC acknowledged the attack. Iran described its strikes as "self-defense" following a US missile that disabled a Botswana-flagged oil tanker heading to an Iranian port. On 2 June, Secretary Rubio told the Senate Foreign Relations Committee that Iran had mined "large segments" of the Strait of Hormuz — the most explicit official public statement of the mine footprint to date — and acknowledged demining could take months. The House passed a war powers resolution on 4 June rebuking Trump's conduct of the Iran campaign, with no immediate operational effect but significant political weight. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span> without move</p>

      <p>F1 (Iran takes a NEW concrete Hormuz closure action by Jun 9) is unchanged at 40%. The Kuwait and Bahrain strikes target military/infrastructure outside Hormuz and do not qualify per F1's resolution criteria, which requires fresh mine-laying, a formally declared total-closure order, or a new IRGC seizure/attack on a commercial vessel. Rubio's mine-footprint confirmation refers to a standing situation, not a new qualifying act. UKMTO and NAVCENT advisories of a new closure action remain NOT_OBSERVED. The Lebanon ceasefire of Jun 3–4 removes one stated trigger for Iran's total closure threat, partially offsetting the ongoing escalation trajectory. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span> without move</p>

      <p>Rubio's Jun 2 testimony confirming demining "takes months" closes F3 (Hormuz ≥50 transits/day by Jun 10) as a NO: at ~4–10 transits per day against a 95/day pre-crisis baseline, with no MOU signed and insurer re-entry contingent on mine clearance, the Jun 10 threshold is physically unreachable in six days. F3 resolves NO at 0.02. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span> (RESOLVED-NO, was 15%)</p>

      <p>F2 (Iran–US written framework signed by Jun 5) expires in under 24 hours with no signed document confirmed. Araghchi stated directly on 4 June that "there is no formal negotiation process underway between Iran and the United States." Neither Trump nor Araghchi's office nor Mojtaba Khamenei's office confirmed a signed document. The 60-day MOU text substantially drafted by 28 May remains unsigned; Trump declined final approval and Iran suspended mediator-channel exchanges on 1 June. F2 resolves NO at 0.04. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> (RESOLVED-NO, was 10%)</p>

      <p>On the de-escalation arc, Israel and Lebanon agreed on 3–4 June to implement a ceasefire following nearly two days of US-mediated negotiations at the State Department. The agreement creates "pilot zones" in southern Lebanon where the Lebanese Armed Forces exercise exclusive control and the IDF withdraws; both parties agreed to reconvene the political and security track in the week of 22 June. Iran's FM Araghchi on 4 June confirmed that Iranian communication channels with the US had not been severed ("messages continue to be exchanged"), in direct contrast to the Tasnim Jun 1 characterization of a total suspension. These two developments — the ceasefire and Araghchi's back-channel confirmation — together constitute the primary upward drivers for F4. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (45%→52%)</p>

      <p>F4's update from 45% to 52% is driven by the removal of Iran's primary stated precondition for resuming talks, partially offset by: Hezbollah's formal acceptance of ceasefire terms being NOT_OBSERVED as of Jun 4; Ben Gvir's public denunciation of the ceasefire as a "grave mistake" on Jun 4; cross-border fire continuing Jun 4; and the US Congress war powers rebuke on Jun 4. Araghchi's "messages continue to be exchanged" characterization does not meet F4's YES resolution threshold, which requires confirmed resumption of a formal negotiation session. F4's source rule is also corrected in today's edition: Iranian state-media removed as a standalone corroborator; the new standard requires two independent TIER1 sources or one TIER1 plus a named Oman/Qatar mediator official on record. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (45%→52%)</p>

      <p>F5 (Hormuz ≥50 transits/day by Jul 2) is reduced from 30% to 22%. F3's NO resolution at the Jun 10 horizon confirms the demining constraint documented by Rubio Jun 2. The recovery chain — deal signed, mines cleared, insurers re-enter, carriers restore scheduled service — requires sequential steps each taking weeks to months; F4's partial improvement at 52% does not provide sufficient confidence that the full chain clears within 28 additional days. NPR's Jun 3 reporting on companies seeking alternative shipping routes confirms behavioral adaptation consistent with an extended closure. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span> (30%→22%)</p>

      <p>F8 is added today as successor to the expired F2: "Will Iran and the US sign or publicly confirm a written MOU/framework agreement before EOD 2026-06-30?" at 35%. The Lebanon ceasefire removes Iran's stated precondition and Araghchi's back-channel confirmation provides a marginal positive, but Araghchi's direct statement of "no formal negotiation process underway" on Jun 4, the ongoing exchange of strikes, and Lebanon ceasefire fragility set a 26-day window for the full chain to close. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>F9 is added today: "Will the Israel–Lebanon ceasefire agreed on 2026-06-04 remain broadly in effect through EOD 2026-06-22?" at 55%. The ceasefire is US-brokered with Israeli cabinet approval and a structured reconvene timeline (week of Jun 22). Against: Hezbollah formal acceptance NOT_OBSERVED; Ben Gvir denounced agreement; cross-border fire continued Jun 4. Sporadic incidents without declared escalation do not resolve F9 NO per its ambiguity rule. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On Israeli domestic politics, no second or third reading of the Knesset dissolution bill was scheduled as of 4 June; the House Committee continued to defer the election date before final readings. Ben Gvir's denunciation of the Lebanon ceasefire on 4 June adds a new friction point but does not change the coalition's seat count or the arithmetic on F6's date negotiation, which remains gated on the Haredi parties' September preference versus Netanyahu's October preference. F6 is unchanged at 50%. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>

      <p>F7 (Netanyahu coalition collapse before Oct 27) is unchanged at 35% under the weekly-cadence rule. No resolution-qualifying event — a Knesset roll-call majority loss or a formal early dissolution announcement — occurred between 2 and 4 June. The dissolution bill's first-reading passage 106–0 on Jun 2 and Ben Gvir's Lebanon ceasefire denunciation on Jun 4 are queued for the Sunday Jun 7 cadence review. → supports <span class="cite">F7</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
