      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (47%):</strong> F1 at 40% drives the floor; Iran's suspended talks and continued strike exchanges mean a new qualifying Hormuz closure action or ceasefire collapse would compound into a broader confrontation, with the F4-fail pathway (48%) adding probability mass even absent a new F1 event.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (43%):</strong> The most probable state: talks resume (F4, 52%) but F8 (MOU signed by Jun 30, 35%) does not close in time, Hormuz remains closed through July, and the Lebanon ceasefire (F9, 55%) holds tenuously while both sides manage the conflict below escalation thresholds.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (10%):</strong> Requires F4 YES + F8 YES + F9 YES + F5 positive trend — the full chain (F4 × F8 × F9 ≈ 0.52 × 0.35 × 0.55 ≈ 10%) — meaning Lebanon ceasefire holds, talks resume, MOU is signed, and Hormuz begins physical recovery before Jul 2.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>Iranian state media on 2–4 June carried a consistent dual-track framing: the regime as both victim of external aggression and a legitimate escalatory actor holding the Strait as leverage in reserve. Tasnim and IRNA continued to characterize Iran's suspension of mediator-channel communications — announced 1 June — as a principled, reversible response to Israeli "ceasefire violations" in Lebanon, not as a strategic breakdown of the diplomatic process. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>After the Kuwait and Bahrain strikes of 3 June, IRGC-affiliated outlets described the operations as "precision self-defense" rather than offensive escalation, citing US attacks on Iranian tankers and the Qeshm Island facility as justification. IRNA quoted Iranian Army spokesperson Brig. Gen. Akraminia warning of "surprising options" if adversaries made another "miscalculation" — a formulation that has appeared in Iranian media approximately every 72 hours during this crisis and functions as deterrence language without committing to a specific operational threshold. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The Bab al-Mandeb threat that Tasnim attached to the Hormuz closure warning on 1 June was absent from Jun 3–4 coverage at comparable intensity. In IRGC-affiliated and IRNA output from Jun 3–4, the secondary-front language did not recur with the same explicit framing as the Jun 1 Tasnim statement. This drop in repetition coincided with the Lebanon ceasefire agreement of Jun 3–4; the absence of the specific phrasing in subsequent coverage is the observable fact, and whether Tehran treated Lebanon's ceasefire as relieving pressure on that secondary front will require further observation to confirm. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>State TV's framing of Araghchi's 4 June comments emphasized that "channels have not been cut off" — a departure from the 1 June Tasnim closure narrative. Iranian practice in past diplomatic cycles has involved managing simultaneously a hardline domestic audience and an available diplomatic off-ramp; the June 4 IRNA/state TV framing follows that pattern and is consistent with Iran preserving optionality rather than committing publicly to either a full resumption or a permanent break. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Coverage of Rubio's Senate testimony on Hormuz mining was either absent from or framed as a US admission of Iranian strategic capability — not as a strategic liability for Tehran. The mine footprint, in Iranian state media treatment, is an asset anchoring Iran's deterrence posture, not a vulnerability that requires explanation or defense. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Lebanon ceasefire agreement itself was covered neutrally in IRNA, with no formal endorsement from Tehran. Mojtaba Khamenei's office had not commented publicly as of 4 June. The absence of a Supreme Leader-office endorsement is consistent with Iran keeping options open on whether to treat the ceasefire as a sufficient precondition for formal talk resumption, rather than committing publicly to either track. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

